\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Three signs in a real spectral triple}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Chamseddine, Connes and Marcolli order the signs of a real
spectral triple by the square of its antiunitary, its commutation
with the self-adjoint Dirac operator, and, in even degrees,
its commutation with the grading
\citet{§2.3, Definition 2.7, (2.8) and table, p. 7, v1}{chamseddine2007gravity}.
In residue order #{0,\ldots,7} their first two rows are
##{\begin{aligned}
&(+,+,-,-,-,-,+,+),\\
&(+,-,+,+,+,-,+,+).
\end{aligned}}
Their grading row at #{0,2,4,6} is #{(+,-,+,-)}.}

\p{For simple spin with coordinate conjugation on the fiber,
the three signs to compare are the square in \ref{fdip-017O},
#{-\eta} on #{\ui D}, and, for #{n=2m}, #{(-1)^m} on the
canonical #{C}. The second sign includes conjugation of #{\ui}, giving
#{\mathcal Q(\ui D_{a,U})\mathcal Q^{-1}
=-\eta\ui D_{a,\bar U}} by \ref{fdip-017R}.
Taking #{\eta=+1} in residues #{1,5} and #{\eta=-1} elsewhere
reproduces those rows on a real background.
For general links the two operators belong to conjugate
backgrounds. Comparing a single operator requires an actual
identification, whose effects on the square and grading enter
as well. With another fiber antiunitary the square on values
is #{c_\eta^2\otimes c_F^2}.}

\p{Their finite example in KO-degree #{6} has square #{+1}
and grading sign #{-1} in (2.19). The following sentence gives
Dirac sign #{+1}. Its multiplicity of three is an input
\citet{§2.4, (2.19), the following sentence and Remark 2.10,
p. 8, v1}{chamseddine2007gravity}.
The definition also uses a represented algebra, its commuting
opposite action and the order-one condition
\citet{§2.3, (2.9)-(2.11), p. 7, v1}{chamseddine2007gravity}.
Matching these three signs concerns one part of that structure.}
